  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Wildlife Without Borders-Western Hemisphere</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">&quot;The community of San Antonio Yaxche is in possession of 8,165 hectares of sub-deciduous tropical forest strategically located in a corridor identified by the National Commission for the Knowledge and Use of Biodiversity as a conservation priority area. Unfortunately, the area is threatened by the expansion of the agriculture frontier and cattle rising. The goal of this project is to maintain the biological connectivity of the region and its unique biodiversity by teaching sustainable economic alternatives to a group of 77 land owners (88% of the population) and their families. The Recipient will achieve this goal by: 1) delivering hands-on training to the families on best practices for intensive agricultural production systems in backyards, raising and handling of domestic animals, production of organic fertilizers, construction and use of biodigestors and wood saving stoves; 2) delivery of two training workshops to community authorities on natural protected areas management, monitoring and patrol, conservation strategies and payment for environmental services; and 3) implementation of an environmental education program for children and youth. .&quot;</narrative>
  </description>
